# PONS $101 — ROB TELEMETRY & MIXED-METHODS RESEARCH PROTOCOL

ROB means:

RESEARCH OBSERVER FOR BOT-LIKE ACTIVITY

ROB is the evidence and telemetry layer of PONS $101.

Its purpose is to observe public blockchain activity surrounding registered token-launch experiments and convert that activity into a reproducible research record.

ROB does not begin with the conclusion:

“THIS WALLET IS A BOT.”

ROB begins with:

WHAT HAPPENED?

WHEN DID IT HAPPEN?

WHAT DOES THE CHAIN PROVE?

WHAT CAN BE CALCULATED?

WHAT CAN ONLY BE INFERRED?

WHAT REMAINS UNKNOWN?

The governing question remains:

WHAT DID WE KNOW BEFORE THE OUTCOME?

==================================================
RESEARCH OBJECTIVE
==================================================

The ROB research program studies whether autonomous, algorithmic, or bot-like market participants discover newly launched tokens without advance promotion and whether their observable behavior can be measured reproducibly.

Primary variables include:

• launch-to-first-trade latency
• launch-to-first-buy latency
• block distance from launch
• transaction ordering
• transaction construction
• wallet recurrence
• trade sizing
• gas behavior
• early entry frequency
• early exit frequency
• fee components
• creator entitlement
• actual treasury receipts
• cross-launch behavioral repetition

ROB studies behavior.

ROB does not attempt to deanonymize participants.

==================================================
PRIMARY RESEARCH QUESTION
==================================================

Do newly launched experimental tokens receive observable trading activity before human-directed public promotion?

==================================================
SECONDARY QUESTIONS
==================================================

1. How quickly does the first trade occur after launch?

2. Does the first trade occur in the same block as launch?

3. How many wallets transact during the opening observation window?

4. Are the same wallets observed across independent launches?

5. Do repeated wallets exhibit similar transaction timing?

6. Do repeated wallets use similar trade sizes?

7. Do repeated wallets exhibit similar entry and exit behavior?

8. What fee components are associated with opening-window trades?

9. Can any additional opening-window fee component be distinguished from ordinary fees?

10. What portion of creator entitlement is actually received by the creator or treasury?

11. How much of the observed behavior is consistent with automation?

12. Which findings replicate across independent experiments?

==================================================
NULL HYPOTHESIS
==================================================

H0:

A newly launched token receiving no advance public promotion will not demonstrate systematically distinguishable automated or bot-like early trading behavior beyond what could reasonably occur through ordinary public discovery and human participation.

==================================================
ALTERNATIVE HYPOTHESIS
==================================================

H1:

A newly launched token receiving no advance public promotion will demonstrate measurable early trading patterns that are consistent with automated discovery or execution.

==================================================
EXPERIMENTAL CONDITION
==================================================

STEALTH / NO-PRE-ANNOUNCEMENT CONDITION

For a registered stealth experiment:

• token metadata remains truthful
• protocol economics remain visible
• material fees remain inspectable
• no artificial liquidity signal is created
• no fake community activity is created
• no wallet is intentionally targeted
• no specific participant is baited
• no pre-launch promotional post is used to intentionally attract traders

The experimental variable is:

ADVANCE PUBLIC PROMOTION = NO

The experiment does not attempt to hide the existence of the token once it becomes publicly visible on-chain.

==================================================
